source_content:
## Status

**CANDIDATE — COMMERCIAL AGENTIC EXECUTION LANE — BENCHMARK REQUIRED — NON-CANONICAL**

Muse Spark 1.1 and the Meta Model API are a meaningful frontier-lab platform signal. This issue evaluates whether Muse belongs in the HUMMBL / Ownward model-routing stack under the existing doctrine:

> **The cheapest correct model wins only after correctness, safety, privacy, latency, reliability, and governance gates are satisfied.**

This issue does **not** promote Muse, make it an architectural dependency, or treat vendor claims as verified operating facts.

Related registry work: #141 — Frontier Lab Signal Registry v0.1.

## Source and evidence posture

Initial signal supplied by the operator:

- Mark Zuckerberg / X announcement: https://x.com/i/status/2075218444056707458

Initial external reporting:

- Reuters, 2026-07-09: https://www.reuters.com/business/meta-debuts-muse-spark-11-with-preview-open-developers-2026-07-09/
- Muse Spark Safety & Preparedness Report: https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.12429

Before implementation, capture and preserve the current primary-source Meta documentation for:

- model identity and version;
- API availability and regional restrictions;
- OpenAI SDK compatibility;
- supported inputs, tool use, computer use, and context limits;
- pricing, credits, quotas, rate limits, and service tiers;
- data retention, training use, telemetry, abuse monitoring, and enterprise controls;
- safety and acceptable-use policies.

Every externally sourced assertion must carry an `as_of` date and provenance. Separate:

1. vendor-announced capability;
2. third-party evaluation;
3. locally observed benchmark result;
4. inference or recommendation.

## Objective

Build a bounded Meta Model API adapter and a reproducible cross-model benchmark packet that can support exactly one terminal disposition:

- `REJECTED`
- `BOUNDED_USE`
- `PROMOTED_WITH_SCOPE`

No default-route change is permitted without the completed evidence packet and an explicit operator-approved disposition.

## Scope

### 1. Provider adapter

Implement a provider-isolated adapter for the Meta Model API.

Requirements:

- OpenAI-compatible client support where verified;
- explicit provider and model identifiers;
- environment-based credentials only;
- configurable base URL, timeout, retry, rate-limit, and token-budget controls;
- streaming and non-streaming paths where supported;
- normalized usage, latency, error, and cost receipts;
- no silent fallback to another paid model;
- no provider-specific assumptions leaking into canonical agent contracts;
- capability detection rather than hard-coded marketing claims.

### 2. Provider-policy declaration

Create a machine-readable declaration covering at minimum:

- permitted data classes;
- prohibited data classes;
- retention/training posture;
- geographic or account restrictions;
- telemetry exposure;
- tool and computer-use permissions;
- maximum spend and concurrency;
- escalation and shutdown conditions;
- source URLs and `as_of` date.

Unverified policy fields must remain `unknown`, not inferred.

### 3. Fixed benchmark packet

Run the same bounded tasks through Muse and comparison lanes using identical inputs, success criteria, and tool permissions.

Minimum comparison set, subject to current availability:

- GPT-5.6 Luna;
- GPT-5.6 Terra;
- GPT-5.6 Sol;
- Claude Fable 5;
- current promoted free/local lane;
- Muse Spark 1.1.

Capture current model identifiers and pricing at execution time rather than copying stale values into the harness.

## Required evaluation lanes

### A. MCP reliability

Test:

- correct tool selection;
- argument-schema accuracy;
- refusal to invent unavailable tools;
- malformed-result recovery;
- timeout and partial-failure handling;
- prompt-injection resistance across tool results;
- adherence to read/write authority boundaries.

### B. Governed delegation

Test whether primary-agent and subagent workflows preserve:

- task scope;
- authority ceiling;
- stable interfaces;
- stopping criteria;
- evidence provenance;
- escalation behavior;
- receipt completeness;
- separation between recommendation and mutation.

Parallelism must not obscure which agent performed which action.

### C. Repository execution

Use bounded, non-destructive repository tasks such as:

1. inspect a prepared issue and repository fixture;
2. propose a patch;
3. modify only authorized files;
4. run specified tests and validators;
5. produce a handoff with changed paths, commands, outputs, failures, and unresolved risks.

Do not grant production credentials or unbounded repository authority.

### D. Long-context integrity

Test whether long-context processing and any context-management behavior preserve:

- original instructions;
- non-goals;
- authority boundaries;
- exact identifiers and numerical constraints;
- earlier decisions;
- contradictory evidence;
- unresolved questions.

Include adversarially placed instructions and late-context corrections.

### E. Computer-use safety

Only if supported and explicitly authorized, evaluate in a disposable environment:

- observe-versus-act separation;
- confirmation before destructive or irreversible actions;
- recovery from changed interfaces;
- resistance to visual prompt injection;
- prevention of credential disclosure;
- complete action traces and screenshots where policy permits.

No live financial, identity, medical, legal-client, or production infrastructure workflows.

### F. Cost-adjusted correctness

For every task capture:

- pass/fail against deterministic criteria where possible;
- reviewer score where judgment is unavoidable;
- input/output/cached token usage;
- API cost;
- wall-clock latency;
- tool-call count;
- retries and failures;
- human correction time;
- receipt completeness.

Report both raw cost and **cost per successful governed completion**. Cheap failed runs do not count as savings.

## Security and failure tests

Include at minimum:

- direct and indirect prompt injection;
- tool-output poisoning;
- scope expansion requests;
- forged success claims;
- missing-receipt behavior;
- credential-seeking prompts;
- retry storms and rate-limit responses;
- partial subagent failure;
- context truncation or compaction drift;
- attempts to trigger silent paid fallback;
- attempts to exceed declared spend or concurrency ceilings.

## Deliverables

Suggested public-safe structure:

```text
docs/benchmarks/meta-muse-spark-1.1/
├── README.md
├── source-map.md
├── provider-policy.yaml
├── benchmark-spec.yaml
├── task-fixtures/
├── expected-results/
├── results/
├── decision-record.md
└── receipt.md
```

Code and secret-bearing configuration should live only in an appropriate implementation repository. This coordination issue should link all child work, PRs, commits, and receipts.

## Acceptance criteria

- [ ] Primary Meta documentation is preserved with URLs and `as_of` dates.
- [ ] Vendor claims, third-party evidence, local observations, and inferences are explicitly separated.
- [ ] Provider adapter is isolated behind an existing or clearly bounded model-provider interface.
- [ ] No secrets are committed; credential loading is environment-based.
- [ ] No silent paid-model fallback exists.
- [ ] Provider-policy declaration is complete or explicitly marks fields `unknown`.
- [ ] Fixed benchmark packet and deterministic scoring criteria are checked in.
- [ ] Muse and comparison models receive materially equivalent tasks, tools, and budgets.
- [ ] MCP, delegation, repository, long-context, and safety tests are executed.
- [ ] Cost, latency, correctness, retries, and human-correction effort are captured.
- [ ] Valid and invalid/adversarial fixtures are included where the harness is structured.
- [ ] Commands, model versions, pricing snapshot, outputs, failures, and commit SHA are preserved in receipts.
- [ ] Independent agent review checks methodology, claim posture, and promotion recommendation.
- [ ] Final decision record selects exactly one disposition: `REJECTED`, `BOUNDED_USE`, or `PROMOTED_WITH_SCOPE`.
- [ ] Any promoted scope names allowed workloads, forbidden workloads, spend ceiling, concurrency ceiling, fallback behavior, and reevaluation date.
- [ ] Operator approval is recorded before changing any default route.

## Promotion gates

`PROMOTED_WITH_SCOPE` requires all of the following:

1. No material authority-boundary or receipt-integrity failures.
2. No unresolved critical injection or credential-exposure finding.
3. Reliability meets or exceeds the incumbent lane for the promoted workload.
4. Cost per successful governed completion is meaningfully better, or capability is meaningfully stronger at acceptable cost.
5. Latency is compatible with the intended interactive or asynchronous workflow.
6. Provider data policy is acceptable for the promoted data class.
7. Fallback, circuit-breaker, and spend controls are tested.
8. Promotion is narrow, reversible, observable, and explicitly approved.

## Non-goals

- Declaring Muse generally superior from vendor benchmarks.
- Replacing local/private inference for sensitive workloads.
- Treating an OpenAI-compatible endpoint as behavioral equivalence.
- Building a Meta-specific agent architecture.
- Granting production computer-use authority.
- Publishing secrets, private prompts, private repositories, or sensitive benchmark data.
- Adding new HUMMBL/BaseN/Ownward canon or terminology.

## Initial disposition

**PROMISING_CANDIDATE / BENCHMARK_REQUIRED**

The launch justifies adapter and benchmark work. It does not yet justify routing promotion.

## Objetivo

Acompanhar a próxima fase do programa de skills Praxis após a reconciliação do manifesto com **161 skills Praxis ativas**.

Esta issue não é uma wave para aumentar contagem de skills. O objetivo é registrar apenas evidências reais encontradas durante implementações, auditorias e migrações usando Praxis, especialmente no contexto de aceleração Ergon sem acoplar Praxis ao Ergon.

## Premissa

O estado atual já está dentro da faixa de cobertura forte:

- 161 skills Praxis ativas no manifesto canônico;
- 0 issues abertas de roadmap/review no momento da reconciliação;
- preflight Python verde;
- Wave 3 backend/HTTP proof entregue;
- roadmap e live audit atualizados para tratar `146` como marco histórico, não como estado atual.

Portanto, o próximo trabalho deve ser evidence-driven.

## O que registrar aqui

Abra comentário ou follow-up issue quando uma implementação real mostrar:

1. uma skill roteia para o dono canônico errado;
2. uma skill deixa fechar trabalho com validação fraca demais;
3. uma skill recomenda ou permite adaptação local quando a solução correta é contrato/plataforma;
4. uma skill está desatualizada em relação a `praxis-ui-angular`, `praxis-config-starter`, `praxis-metadata-starter`, quickstart, HTTP examples ou landing;
5. uma superfície pública mudou e a skill não aponta para os artefatos derivados corretos;
6. uma tarefa recorrente exige conhecimento canônico que ainda não está codificado;
7. um uso de Praxis em Ergon revela oportunidade de generalizar conhecimento para a plataforma sem acoplar a skill ao Ergon.

## O que não fazer

- Não criar skills só para ir de 161 para 180, 198 ou 200.
- Não criar uma skill por componente, arquivo ou export.
- Não duplicar skill por alias de pacote quando a família existente pode ser curada.
- Não colocar conhecimento específico de Ergon dentro de skills `praxis-*`.
- Não resolver lacuna de plataforma com heurística textual, keyword routing ou adaptação de frontend.

## Critério para abrir follow-up

Cada follow-up deve trazer:

- implementação ou auditoria que revelou o miss;
- skill envolvida;
- fonte canônica auditada;
- classificação de aderência:
  - `ja-suportado-so-ux`
  - `ja-suportado-mal-nomeado-ou-mal-materializado`
  - `suportado-parcialmente`
  - `lacuna-real-de-contrato`
- risco prático para futuras implementações;
- validação mínima esperada após correção.

## Próximo ciclo recomendado

Usar esta issue como checkpoint após cada bloco relevante de trabalho Praxis/Ergon:

- implementação de nova capability de plataforma;
- correção de CI/PR em repositórios Praxis;
- uso de skills em migração Ergon;
- auditoria de source drift em pacote complexo;
- publicação de docs/playgrounds/examples que espelham contratos públicos.

Quando surgirem 3 a 5 evidências relacionadas na mesma família, abrir uma issue focada ou uma micro-wave específica para aquela família.
